   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Introduction <a class=\"anchor\" id=\"c1\"></a>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Most digital image files are represented as 2-dimensional matrices of 'pixels'. Each pixel itself is an array of 3 integers ranging from 0 to 255, representing the intensity of the <font color=\"red\"><b>R</b></font>ed, <font color=\"green\"><b>G</b></font>reen and <font color=\"blue\"><b>B</b></font>lue channels.\n",
    "\n",
    "Some images have a fourth channel called the *alpha channel*, where a value of 0 is visualized as **black** and renders the pixel that underlies it completely **transparent**, while a value of 255 is visualized as **white** and renders the pixel that underlies it completely **opaque**. If an image consists of a single channel of blacks, grays and whites, it is considered a *grayscale* image.\n",
    "\n",
    "All methods in this notebook are capable of processing images in grayscale and/or have alpha channels, with the alpha channel intact in the output:\n",
    "- For grayscale images, each 'pixel' in the matrix is converted into an array of 3 elements to simulate an RGB image prior to processing, allowing it to be worked with like a regular 3-channel, RGB image.\n",
    "- For images with an alpha channel, the alpha channel is separated before processing, and then 'reapplied' onto the proccessed RGB matrix to restore the original transparency of the pixels. This step is omitted for the blurring, sharpening, flipping and cropping methods, since they do not require the input array to be three-dimensional.\n",
    "\n",
    "The processing methods in this notebook include:\n",
    "1. Adjusting brightness\n",
    "2. Adjusting contrast\n",
    "3. Color filters (grayscale & sepia)\n",
    "5. Flipping (on both axes)\n",
    "6. Blurring\n",
    "7. Sharpening\n",
    "8. Cropping (box crop, circle crop and double-ellipse crop)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Implementation <a class=\"anchor\" id=\"c2\"></a>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The libraries needed for this notebook include **NumPy** for processing the image matrix and **Matplotlib** to visualize the output."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 1,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "import numpy as np\n",
    "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The processing methods will be demonstrated on an image of a colorful macaw. The original image file is included with the repository containing this notebook."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 2,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "text/plain": [
       "((256, 256, 3), <matplotlib.image.AxesImage at 0x19e34a21430>)"
      ]
     },
     "execution_count": 2,
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "execute_result"
    },
    {
