  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Being a JPG file, the input image does not have an alpha channel. In order to handle images with one, let's define a helper method to split the alpha channel from the image and return it, along with the RGB matrix:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 4,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "def split_alpha(img):\n",
    "    alpha = img[:, :, 3]\n",
    "    img = img[:, :, :3]\n",
    "    return img, alpha"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Let's also define a method to neatly present images side-by-side, to compare our befores and afters: "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 33,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "def visualize(images, titles=[]):\n",
    "    fig = plt.figure(figsize=(len(images) * 2.5, 2.5))\n",
    "    # plot the images\n",
    "    for i in range(len(images)):\n",
    "        fig.add_subplot(1, len(images), i + 1)\n",
    "        plt.imshow(images[i], cmap='gray', vmin=0, vmax=1)\n",
    "        if (titles):\n",
    "            plt.title(titles[i])\n",
    "    fig.tight_layout()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "#### Brightness <a class=\"anchor\" id=\"c21\"></a>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "To adjust an image's brightness, we can add or subtract a specific value from each pixel. The `brightness` method takes the pixel matrix and `amount` (an integer within  $[-100, 100]$) as parameters. `amount` represents the **percentage** of the output image's brightness compared to the input image. By multiplying the input matrix with `amount` and add the resulting matrix to the original matrix, then clipping the pixels' value range to $[0, 1]$, we can effectively alter its brightness."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 31,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "def brightness(img, amount=50):\n",
    "    # grayscale images only have 2 dimensions\n",
    "    # manually add a third axis if the image is grayscale\n",
    "    if len(img.shape) == 2:\n",
    "        img = img[..., np.newaxis]\n",
    "    # extract the alpha channel if it exists\n",
    "    if img.shape[2] > 3:\n",
    "        rgb, alpha = split_alpha(img)\n",
    "    # otherwise, just duplicate the original image\n",
    "    else:\n",
    "        rgb = img.copy()\n",
    "    output = rgb + (rgb * (amount / 100))\n",
    "    output = np.clip(output, 0., 1.) \n",
    "    # if the original image has an alpha channel, stack it back on\n",
    "    if img.shape[2] > 3:\n",
    "        return np.dstack((output, alpha))\n",
    "    \n",
    "    return output"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 34,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 750x250 with 3 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "brightened = brightness(orig, 50)\n",
    "darkened = brightness(orig, -50)\n",
    "visualize([orig, brightened, darkened], ['Original image', '+50% brightness', '-50% brightness'])"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "#### Contrast <a class=\"anchor\" id=\"c22\"></a>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "To adjust an image's contrast, we 'stretch' or 'compress' the value range of each pixel. In other words, to increase the contrast of an image, we bring the 'bright' pixels' values closer to $1$ and the 'dark' pixels' values closer to $0$ and vice versa. Exponential functions are capable of this operation, in which an exponent less than $1$ will increase the contrast and vice versa.\n",
    "\n",
    "Adjusting an image's contrast by passing the matrix through an exponential function only affects the pixels whose values are at the extreme ends of the range, so *midtones*, whose values are around $0.5$, are virtually unaffected. To counter this, all the pixels will have their value shifted to the left (along the $x$ axis) by $0.5$ prior to processing. However, this introduces negative values that cannot be plugged into exponential functions. We can process the absolute value of these pixels before multiplying them with the corresponding value from the *sign array* return by **NumPy**'s `sign` method. The pixels are then shifted back to ensure the value range remains $[0,1]$.\n",
    "\n",
    "Further reading on how this work is included in the [References](#c3) section.\n",
    "\n",
    "The `contrast` method takes the pixel matrix and `amount` (an integer within  $[-100, 100]$) as parameters. `amount` represents the **percentage** of the output image's contrast compared to the input image. It calculates the contrast `factor` using the formula $$factor = \\frac{100 + amount}{100}$$ The left-shifted matrix is then lifted to the power of $\\frac{1}{factor}$ and shifted back to the right before returning."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 8,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "def contrast(img, amount=100):\n",
    "    if len(img.shape) == 2:\n",
    "        img = img[..., np.newaxis]\n",
    "    if img.shape[2] > 3:\n",
    "            rgb, alpha = split_alpha(img)\n",
    "    else:\n",
    "        rgb = img.copy()\n",
    "    # calculate contrast factor\n",
    "    factor = 1 + (amount / 100)\n",
    "    # 1 / factor is invalid if factor is 0 (or amount is -100)\n",
    "    # returns a gray image\n",
    "    if factor == 0:\n",
    "        return np.full_like(rgb, 0.5)\n",
    "    # shifting midtones (values ~ 0.5) towards 0 to drastically change midtones\n",
    "    shifted = (rgb - 0.5) * 2\n",
    "    # after shifting, shadows now have negative values\n",
    "    # obtain their absolute value to raise them to the power of 1 / factor\n",
    "    # then retrieve their original sìgns by multiplying with the sign array\n",
    "    output = np.abs(shifted) ** (1 / factor) * np.sign(shifted)\n",
    "    # recenter the midtones and shifting all shadows to non-negative values\n",
    "    output = (output / 2) + 0.5\n",
    "    if img.shape[2] > 3:\n",
    "        return np.dstack((output, alpha))\n",
    "    return output"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 35,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 750x250 with 3 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "hi_contrast = contrast(orig, 70)\n",
    "lo_contrast = contrast(orig, -70)\n",
    "visualize([orig, hi_contrast, lo_contrast], ['Original image', '+70% contrast', '-70% contrast'])"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "#### Color filters <a class=\"anchor\" id=\"c23\"></a>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "##### Grayscale filter"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The CIE 1931 color space defines the formula to convert a pixel with linear RGB values to a grayscale pixel such that its linear luminance stays the same as follows:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$Y_{linear} = 0.2126R_{linear} + 0.7152G_{linear} + 0.7222B_{linear}$$\n",
    "\n",
    "The `desaturate` method performs a matrix multiplication for a matrix consisting of the above coefficients and the pixel matrix of the input image. The resulting matrix will not have values exceeding the $[0, 1]$ range, since the sum of the coefficients is $1$.\n",
    "\n",
    "If the input image has an alpha channel, the method will stack the resulting matrix three times before laying the alpha channel on top (an image with an alpha channel must have four channels). Otherwise, the method returns a single-channel image."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 10,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "def desaturate(img):\n",
    "    if len(img.shape) == 2:\n",
    "        img = img[..., np.newaxis]\n",
    "    if img.shape[2] > 3:\n",
    "        rgb, alpha = split_alpha(img)\n",
    "    else:\n",
    "        rgb = img.copy()\n",
    "    # make a weights matrix based on the CIE 1931 linear luminance\n",
    "    weights = np.array([0.2126, 0.7152, 0.0722])\n",
    "    output = np.matmul(rgb[...,:3], weights)\n",
    "    # in order to retain the alpha channel, image must have 3 color channels\n",
    "    # stack the gray channel 3 times\n",
    "    if img.shape[2] > 3:\n",
    "        return np.dstack((output, output, output, alpha))\n",
    "    return output"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "##### Sepia filter"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The formulae to calculate new RGB values for each sepia-filtered pixel are as follows:\n",
    "$$R_{sepia} = 0.393R + 0.769G + 0.189B\n",
    "  B_{sepia} = 0.349R + 0.686G + 0.168B\n",
    "  G_{sepia} = 0.272R + 0.534G + 0.131B$$\n",
    "\n",
    "The `sepia` method performs a matrix multiplication for a matrix consisting of the above coefficients and the pixel matrix of the input image. The resulting matrix is then clipped to range before being returned."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 11,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "def sepia(img):\n",
    "    if len(img.shape) == 2:\n",
    "        img = img[..., np.newaxis]\n",
    "    if img.shape[2] > 3:\n",
    "        rgb, alpha = split_alpha(img)\n",
    "    else:\n",
    "        rgb = img.copy()\n",
    "    # new_color = R_weight * R + G_weight * G + B_weight * B\n",
    "    sep_filter = np.array([[.393, .769, .189],\n",
    "                           [.349, .686, .168],\n",
    "                           [.272, .534, .131]])\n",
    "    # transpose the filter to align red weights with the red channel, etc.\n",
    "    output = np.matmul(rgb[...,:3], sep_filter.T)\n",
    "    output = np.clip(output, 0., 1.)\n",
    "    # stack the alpha channel back on if it exists\n",
    "    if img.shape[2] > 3:\n",
    "        return np.dstack((output, alpha))\n",
    "    return output"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 36,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "